    "clos": [
      {
        "no": "1",
        "clo_name": "CLO1",
        "clo_details": "Manipulate logical expressions and use logic for inference\nLO1.1 Determine the truth value of a proposition; check if two propositions are equivalent; translate between logical expressions and sentences for propositional logic.\nLO1.2 Determine the truth value of a proposition; check if two propositions are equivalent; translate between logical expressions and sentences for predicate logic.\nLO1.3 Manipulate logical expressions with nested quantifiers.\nLO1.4 Recognize the inference rules used in a valid statemment; use inference rules to draw conclusions; check if a statement is valid."
      },
      {
        "no": "2",
        "clo_name": "CLO2",
        "clo_details": "Perform basic operations on sets, functions and sequences\nLO2.1 Distinguish basic terminologies in set theory; find the power set and cartersian product; represent a subset using binary bits.\nLO2.2 Perform operations on sets; check if two sets are equal.\nLO2.3 Determine if a given rule represents a function; perform operations on functions and manipulate functions inlvolving the floor and the ceiling functions.\nLO2.4 Determine if a function is one-to-one, onto or a bijection, and describe how these properties can be used to compare two sets.\nLO2.5 Find a formula for a sequence; evaluate a finite sum with a possible use of special summations."
      },
      {
        "no": "3",
        "clo_name": "CLO3",
        "clo_details": "Analyze basic algorithms written in pseudo codes and determine their complexities\nLO3.1 Determine if a procedure is an algorithm; work out basic algorithms written in pseudocodes including searching, sorting and greedy algorithms.\nLO3.2 Find big-O, big-Omega and big-Theta estimates of a function; describe complexity of an algorithm."
      },
      {
        "no": "4",
        "clo_name": "CLO4",
        "clo_details": "Discuss applications of modular arithmetic and analyze algorithms on integers\nLO4.1 Perform integer division and modular arithmetic; apply congruence in generating pseudorandom numbers and in cryptology.\nLO4.2 Find the base b expansion of an integer; perform binary operations and modular exponentiation on integers.\nLO4.3 Check if a number is prime and find prime factorization of an integer; find greatest common divisor and least common multiple."
      },
      {
        "no": "5",
        "clo_name": "CLO5",
        "clo_details": "Use induction and recursive definition to prove a statement or to describe an object, and write recursive algorithms\nLO5.1 Prove mathematical statements by induction.\nLO5.2 Manipulate sequences and sets defined by recursion.\nLO5.3 Work out recursive algorithms, including the mergesort algorithm; distinguish between iterative and recursive algorithms."
      },
      {
        "no": "6",
        "clo_name": "CLO6",
        "clo_details": "Solve counting problems\nLO6.1 Use basic rules for counting\nLO6.2 Use recurrence relations for counting, including divide-and-conquer recurrence relations."
      },
      {
        "no": "7",
        "clo_name": "CLO7",
        "clo_details": "Analyze the structure of a graph and find shortest paths in a weighted graph\nLO7.1 Classify and describe a graph; use hand-shaking theorem to analyze a graph; identify special graphs including bipartite graphs.\nLO7.2 Represent a graph using incidence and adjacency matrices; determine if two graphs are isomorphic.\nLO7.3 Check connectivity of a graph and identify its connected components; count paths in a graph.\nLO7.4 Determine if a graph has Euler/Hamilton paths/circuits; find Euler and Hamilton paths/circuits in a graph.\nLO7.5 Run Dijsktra's algorithm to determine the shortest paths and the shortest distance in a weighted graph."
      },
      {
        "no": "8",
        "clo_name": "CLO8",
        "clo_details": "Analyze the structure of a tree and describe algorithms using trees\nLO8.1 Check if a graph is a tree, and identify basic terminologies of a tree; use properties of m-ary trees to solve problems.\nLO8.2 Build binary search trees and decision trees; analyze complexities of algorithms described using binary search trees and decision trees.\nLO8.3 Recognize and construct prefix codes; build optimal trees using Huffman coding.\nLO8.4 Traverse a tree and use tree traversal to generate notations; evaluate prefix and postfix expressions.\nLO8.5 Run Depth-first search and Breadth-first search algorithms to find spanning trees\nLO8.6 Find minimum spanning trees using Prim, Kruskal,... algorithms"
      }
    ],
